\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
Pairwise-comparison leaderboards rank models, teams and players from who beat whom: Chatbot Arena fits Bradley--Terry (BT) scores to crowd votes on anonymous model pairs~\citep{chiang2024}, MT-Bench compares answers with expert and GPT-4 judgments~\citep{zheng2023}, and sports ratings do the same with game outcomes. Their published uncertainty is a bootstrap confidence interval. An interval answers whether another sample from the same process would reorder the models. It does not answer two questions that a reader, a model developer or an adversary asks of the \emph{given} data: how many of the existing comparisons carry the conclusion ``$a$ is above $b$'', and whether that number is large or small for a dataset of this size and spread. Measuring the first is an influence analysis~\citep{koh2017,broderick2020}; the second needs a reference, because the raw number is not comparable between datasets. A pair separated by $z$ standard errors needs a number of alterations that grows with $z$ and with the square root of the sample size, so that a share of 0.2\% of Arena's votes and a share of 3\% of a basketball season's games may be equally fragile.

We make the number comparable and ask whether real data depart from what Bradley--Terry statistics alone predict. For each conclusion we find the smallest number of deletions, or of changed outcomes, after which the \emph{exact} refit reverses it, as a verified upper bound ($k^{*}$, Section~\ref{sec:method}); we divide the alterations' effect by the standard error to obtain a quantity in standard-error units; and we compare each dataset with data simulated from its own fitted model on the same comparison graph, with the same estimator and search. Whether a dataset is equivalent to this same-graph reference is decided by a margin fixed beforehand from synthetic data. We apply this to all pairs of \nSets{} datasets of four evaluation designs: crowd votes, expert labels, LLM judges re-labelling the same battles, and sports seasons.

\paragraph{What we find.} First, the count per unit of separation follows the same-graph reference: real datasets of all four designs lie within the margin of it in nearly every case, as a first-order identity makes largely expected, the exceptions being a few rows with too few pairs and a small offset of the method that shows in Arena outcome flips (Section~\ref{sec:headline}). Second, noise and an adversary differ by orders of magnitude: random alterations of the size of the smallest targeted change essentially never reverse a conclusion, while a pair that the published intervals separate loses its separation under a targeted change of a few tenths of a percent to about one percent of the votes in the large crowd and judge datasets (Section~\ref{sec:survival}). Third, the comparison with the reference has limited power: in a simulated control it did not respond to the departures tried, so close agreement is not evidence that Bradley--Terry fits, and the one design effect we find is in the published interval, about \seRatioExpert{} times the model-based one for MT-Bench expert labels (Section~\ref{sec:design}, Appendix~\ref{app:control}). We add a regression that predicts $k^{*}$ from separation, size, density and ties, no better than the dataset's own simulation and worse for held-out designs (Section~\ref{sec:rule}), and checks that one-shot influence ranking, the usual approximation, finds the smallest change in only part of the conclusions (Section~\ref{sec:search}).

\paragraph{Contribution.} It is a measurement and calibration paper, not a new attack. The scale, the same-graph reference, the comparison of an adversary with noise at equal budget, the matched contrasts of crowd, judge and expert labels on identical items, the prediction of $k^{*}$ and the checks of the search are ours as far as we could consult the literature; one listed paper on dropping preferences from Arena-style rankings was left unread (Section~\ref{sec:related}), so we claim no priority for counting the dropped comparisons that reverse a ranking. The scaled quantity is near one largely by a first-order identity (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}); we use it as a gauge and state conclusions about departures from the reference. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} states what the data cannot say; the code and the analysis plan are in Appendix~\ref{app:repro}.
